  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct absolute oral bioavailability measurement for the query molecule is present in the supplied records.",
    "No IV-comparator or matched oral-versus-IV exposure data for the query; transfer from steroid analogs is structural class-level rather than exact-condition.",
    "Rolipram records show large species differences in F, so animal F values for analogs may not predict human F for the query.",
    "High logD 5.54 and molecular weight 475 may limit solubility; this is not experimentally resolved."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "L2 records from the same steroid contexts add quantitative absolute and relative F values all above 20% (estetrol 70% or more in rats and about 33% in mice; 17-methyltestosterone 97-101% relative in humans and 37% absolute in rats; vixotrigine 42-68% in animals), reinforcing the high prediction. The rolipram context shows species-dependent F but its human gold is 73% and it is not steroid-like, so it does not contradict. Confidence remains moderate because no direct query-specific F data exist and the query is highly lipophilic."
}

